Audit Service UML Class Diagram — Explanatory Note
1. Purpose

The Audit Service owns the SecureCloud application-level security and audit trail.

It does not own users, credentials, conversations, messages, files, file metadata, delivery state, or infrastructure state. Its domain ownership is limited to immutable audit records.

The runtime producer model is:

Gateway
Auth Service
Messaging Service
Files Service
        │
        ▼
Audit Service
        │
        ▼
ClickHouse

Infrastructure and deployment components are explicitly not Audit Service producers. Infrastructure logging, metrics and traces remain separate observability concerns.

2. The central domain class: AuditEvent

Unlike Auth, Messaging and Files, the Audit Service has one central immutable domain record:

AuditEvent

It directly corresponds to the approved ClickHouse audit_events model:

event_id
occurred_at
received_at
event_type
severity
producer_service
producer_instance
actor_type
actor_id
target_type
target_id
correlation_id
causation_id
event_data

The concrete approved ClickHouse table uses MergeTree, monthly partitioning by occurred_at, chronological ordering, and a 36-month TTL.

The important conceptual difference is:

AuditEvent
    │
    ├── immutable
    ├── append-only
    └── never normally updated

There is no updateAuditEvent() operation.

If something changes, a new event is created.

3. eventId and idempotency

eventId is generated by the producer using UUIDv7.

It provides:

distributed generation;
global uniqueness;
time-ordering characteristics;
logical event identity.

Most importantly:

event_id
    =
logical idempotency identifier

If an Outbox publisher retries the same logical event, it must reuse the same eventId.

Therefore:

Original event
event_id = E123
        ↓
Audit submission fails
        ↓
Retry
event_id = E123

It must not generate:

Retry
event_id = E999

because that would make the retry appear to be a new event.

4. AuditIngestionHandler

This is the Audit Service transport boundary.

Conceptually:

Authenticated runtime service
        │
        ▼
AuditIngestionHandler

It coordinates the first stage of ingestion:

authenticate producer;
validate the event;
check normal retry duplicates;
enqueue accepted events into the bounded ingestion pipeline.

It does not directly insert every received event into ClickHouse synchronously.

That would contradict the approved asynchronous ingestion architecture.

5. ProducerAuthenticationService

Only authenticated runtime services may submit audit events.

The producer identity contains:

producerService
producerInstance

For example:

producerService  = MESSAGING
producerInstance = messaging-4

The Audit Service authenticates the producer according to the approved service-to-service security architecture before accepting its event.

This service deliberately does not authenticate users.

That responsibility remains with Auth.

The Audit Service only needs to authenticate:

runtime service
        ↓
Audit Service

communication.

6. Validation layer

The UML separates validation into three conceptual responsibilities.

AuditEventValidator

The main validation coordinator.

AuditEventSchemaValidator

Checks that the event conforms to the canonical Audit contract.

Examples:

valid event_type;
required identifiers;
valid severity;
required timestamps;
valid actor/target structure.
SensitiveDataValidator

Ensures that prohibited sensitive content does not enter Audit.

The Audit Service must never store:

passwords
password hashes
access tokens
refresh tokens
JWT values
MFA secrets
private keys
session keys
plaintext messages
decrypted message payloads
plaintext files
decrypted file content

Audit records security facts and bounded metadata, not secrets or protected application content.

7. AuditDeduplicationService

Audit delivery is intentionally:

At-least-once

Therefore duplicate delivery can occur because of:

lost acknowledgements;
network failures;
producer restart;
Audit Service restart.

The MVP solution is application-level deduplication.

The approved configuration is:

Recent event ID cache:
100,000 event IDs

TTL:
10 minutes

Conceptually:

Incoming event
      │
      ▼
event_id already in cache?
      │
   ┌──┴──┐
   │     │
  Yes    No
   │     │
duplicate accept
retry       ↓
        enqueue event

ClickHouse is not treated as a traditional database with a normal unique constraint enforcing this retry behavior.

8. RecentEventIdCache

This class represents the bounded, short-lived in-memory retry deduplication cache.

Its responsibilities are deliberately limited:

contains(eventId)
insert(eventId)
evictExpired()

It is not authoritative audit storage.

The authoritative store remains ClickHouse.

This is important because:

Deduplication cache
        ≠
Audit database

The cache prevents common retry duplicates; longer-term duplicate detection remains possible analytically through event_id.

9. Bounded ingestion pipeline

The approved ingestion pipeline is:

Authenticated Producers
        │
        ▼
Bounded Ingestion Queue
        │
        ▼
Validation Workers
        │
        ▼
Batch Builder
        │
        ▼
Bounded Insert Workers
        │
        ▼
ClickHouse

The UML maps this directly to:

AuditIngestionQueue
        ↓
ValidationWorker
        ↓
BatchBuilder
        ↓
ClickHouseInsertWorker
        ↓
AuditEventRepository

The concrete MVP configuration is:

Validation workers:       4
Insert workers:           2
Maximum queued events:    10,000

The queue is globally bounded.

There is deliberately no unbounded queue per producer.

10. AuditIngestionQueue and backpressure

AuditIngestionQueue represents bounded memory.

This is important because audit events must not cause uncontrolled memory growth:

Producers faster than ClickHouse
            │
            ▼
unbounded queue
            │
            ▼
memory exhaustion

Instead:

Queue full
    │
    ▼
Backpressure
    │
    ▼
Producer Outbox remains durable
    │
    ▼
Publisher retries later

The Audit Service does not silently drop security events.

11. BatchBuilder

ClickHouse performs best with batched inserts rather than one database operation per event.

The approved MVP batching policy is:

Maximum batch size:
1,000 events

Maximum batching delay:
100 milliseconds

Therefore:

BatchBuilder
     │
     ├── 1,000 events reached
     │
     └── OR 100 ms elapsed
               │
               ▼
        AuditEventBatch
               │
               ▼
      ClickHouseInsertWorker

The batch is bounded both by event count and waiting time.

12. ClickHouseInsertWorker

This class represents the bounded asynchronous persistence workers.

The approved MVP starts with:

2 insert workers

Their responsibility is:

AuditEventBatch
        ↓
AuditEventRepository
        ↓
ClickHouse

They are not one worker per producer or one thread per event.

This keeps resource usage bounded.

13. AuditEventRepository

AuditEventRepository is the persistence abstraction.

The implementation:

ClickHouseAuditEventRepository

owns communication with the Audit Service's authoritative ClickHouse database.

The key architectural rule is:

Gateway
Auth
Messaging
Files
    │
    │ do NOT write directly
    ▼
Audit ClickHouse tables

All writes go through the Audit Service.

The Audit Service exclusively owns audit persistence.

14. ClickHouseClient

This abstraction represents the selected C++ ClickHouse client implementation.

The class diagram intentionally does not choose a concrete C++ library yet.

The architectural responsibility is:

insert(table, rows)
query(statement)

The actual C++ implementation can later use the selected ClickHouse client library without changing the service's domain responsibilities.

15. ClickHouse physical model

The repository persists to:

audit_events

using the approved design:

ENGINE = MergeTree

PARTITION BY
toYYYYMM(occurred_at)

ORDER BY
(
    occurred_at,
    producer_service,
    event_type,
    event_id
)

TTL
occurred_at + INTERVAL 36 MONTH

This matches the Audit workload:

append-heavy
+
time-oriented
+
filtered investigation
+
analytical queries

The data model explicitly selected MergeTree because audit events are immutable and append-oriented.

16. AuditQueryService

Audit is not only an ingestion service.

It also supports investigation queries.

The approved MVP query patterns are:

Time range
Producer + time range
Event type + time range
Severity + time range
Actor + time range
Target + time range
Correlation ID

The UML therefore keeps query logic separate:

AuditQueryHandler
        ↓
AuditQueryService
        ↓
AuditEventRepository
        ↓
ClickHouse

The service does not create a separate table for each investigation pattern.

The existing MergeTree ordering is optimized primarily for chronological investigation.

17. Actor and target

AuditEvent contains both:

actor

and:

target

These answer different questions.

Actor

Who performed or initiated the action?

actor_type
actor_id

Supported types include:

USER
DEVICE
SERVICE
SYSTEM
UNKNOWN
Target

What entity was affected?

target_type
target_id

Initial target types are:

USER
DEVICE
CONVERSATION
FILE
SERVICE

For example:

User A revokes Device X

Actor:
USER / User A

Target:
DEVICE / Device X

All IDs remain opaque.

The Audit Service does not create foreign keys into Auth, Messaging or Files databases.

18. Correlation and causation
correlationId

Identifies a larger distributed operation.

For example:

Client operation
      │
      ▼
Gateway
      │
      ▼
Auth
      │
      ▼
Messaging

The related events can share one:

correlation_id

This supports reconstruction of distributed activity.

causationId

Optionally identifies the event that directly caused another event.

For example:

Event A
auth.login.attempt

      │
      ▼

Event B
auth.mfa.challenge.created

causation_id = Event A

Causation remains optional in the approved MVP model.

19. AuditInternalEventService

The Audit Service itself may generate internal application audit events.

Examples approved in the model include:

audit.event.rejected
audit.producer.authentication.failed

These events remain within the Audit Service domain.

Conceptually:

Audit Service detects
producer authentication failure
        ↓
AuditInternalEventService
        ↓
Audit ingestion pipeline
        ↓
ClickHouse

This avoids creating a special side-channel persistence path for Audit's own security events.

20. What is deliberately absent from the class diagram

To remain aligned with the approved MVP, the diagram deliberately does not include:

AuditEventUpdater
AuditEventDeleter
PerEventHashChain
CheckpointRepository
CheckpointSigner

The reason is important.

The Audit Service MVP does not implement per-event cryptographic hash chains.

Periodic cryptographic checkpoints are a future enhancement, not part of the current implementation model.

The approved future conceptual model is:

Audit events for time period
        ↓
Canonical cryptographic digest
        ↓
Cryptographically signed checkpoint

But introducing these classes now would incorrectly suggest they are MVP implementation requirements.

